Module 3 - Lesson 2: Reflection

1. Why does the application use SQLite instead of relying only on a Dart list? 
A Dart list is stored in memory, so it is erased when the app closes. 
SQLite saves the tasks in a file on the device, so they are still there when the app is opened 
again.

2. What is the purpose of the primary key in the tasks table? 
The primary key (id) gives every task its own unique number. Even if two tasks have the 
same title, the app can still tell them apart. My code uses the id to update or 
delete exactly one task.

3. How does the repository simplify the UI's interaction with the database? 
The UI only calls simple methods like create(), getAll(), markCompleted(), and delete(). 
It does not need to know any SQL. All the database code is in one place (TaskRepository), 
so the screen stays clean and easier to read.

4. What would happen if a task were removed from the displayed list but not deleted from SQLite? 
The task would look deleted, but its record would still be in the database. 
When the app loads the tasks again, the task would come back.

5. If the database schema later needed a new column for task priority, what parts of the application might need to change?

The CREATE TABLE statement, to add the priority column.
The database version, plus an onUpgrade migration so old users keep their data.
The repository, so it can save and read the priority.
The UI, so users can enter and see the priority.